15
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:48,540
Every American schoolchild learns
about the great Chicago fire and Mrs.

16
00:00:48,820 --> 00:00:49,820
O 'Leary's cow.

17
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:53,920
Far fewer have ever heard the name
Pestigo, even though the Wisconsin

18
00:00:53,960 --> 00:00:58,760
Firestorm killed an estimated 1200
to 2500 people in a single night,

19
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:02,240
making it the deadliest wildfire
in recorded American history.

20
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:08,000
Look across Lake Michigan on a map
of October 8, 1871, and the mystery

21
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:09,080
deepens even further.

22
00:01:10,020 --> 00:01:12,980
Chicago was not alone.
Pestigo was not alone.

23
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:18,380
Whole towns across Michigan, Holland,
Manistee, and Port Huron were burning at

24
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:22,840
the very same hour. Why did the entire
Upper Midwest ignite on one Sunday

25
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:23,880
night?

26
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:27,180
And how was America's deadliest
fire nearly erased from memory?

27
00:01:30,060 --> 00:01:34,360
To understand what happened on October
8, you have to step back to the summer

28
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:39,560
of 1871. Across Illinois, Wisconsin,
and Michigan, the rains simply

29
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:43,880
stopped falling. Between July and
October, the region received barely a

30
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:45,060
quarter of its normal rainfall.

31
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Wells ran dry,

32
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:51,260
Pete Swamps turned into crumbly brown
dust, and wooden boardwalks cracked in

33
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:55,260
the heat. Up in northeastern Wisconsin
and across Michigan, booming lumber

34
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:59,120
towns had spent decades clear-cutting
towering white pines to build Chicago

35
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:00,220
and the growing frontier.

36
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:05,260
Behind them lay millions of acres of
dried treetops, bark and branches known

37
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:09,980
as logging slash, piled 10 feet deep
along rail lines and farm clearings.

38
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:14,880
Settlers routinely used small brush fires
to clear farmland, and for weeks the

39
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:16,880
northern woods hung thick with smoky haze.

40
00:02:17,580 --> 00:02:21,180
Meanwhile, Chicago itself was
practically a forest stood on end.

41
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:25,040
Two -thirds of its buildings were pine
lumber, linked by wooden sidewalks,

42
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:27,380
wooden bridges, and shingle roofs.

43
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:30,320
All three states were
loaded like dry gunpowder.

44
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:33,540
All they needed was a match
and a wind from the plains.

45
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:38,580
On Sunday afternoon, October 8, a deep
low-pressure weather system swept

46
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:42,960
northeast out of the great plains,
whipping fierce bone-dry southwest winds

47
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,000
across Lake Michigan.

48
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:48,740
That single -weather front turned hundreds
of smoldering brush fires, and one

49
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:51,960
spark in a Chicago alley into
a multi-state catastrophe.

50
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:59,400
Around 9 o 'clock that Sunday night,
flames broke out inside a wooden barn

51
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:03,940
behind Patrick and Catherine O 'Leary's
cottage on Dukovin Street on Chicago's

52
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:08,660
southwest side. Exhausted firefighters
had just spent 17 hours battling

53
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:10,540
another major fire the night before,

54
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:14,800
and when the watchmen misread the location
of the new blaze, precious minutes

55
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:19,720
slipped away. Driven by fierce southwest
winds, burning planks sailed across

56
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:23,960
the Chicago River like flaming spears,
igniting the downtown business district

57
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:25,940
and leaping again into the north side.

58
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:30,540
When the city's wooden waterworks roof
collapsed, the water mains went dry.

59
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:35,940
Over the next 30 hours, until rain
finally arrived early Tuesday morning, the

60
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:41,320
fire gutted roughly 3.3 square miles,
destroying over 17,000 buildings,

61
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:46,180
leaving about 100,000 residents
homeless, and claiming roughly 300 lives.

62
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:50,180
Among the very few public landmarks
left standing in the ashes was the

63
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:54,460
limestone Chicago Water Tower, which
still rises on Michigan Avenue today.

64
00:03:55,160 --> 00:03:59,380
Almost immediately, newspapers looked
for a scapegoat and blamed Catherine O

65
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:01,320
'Leary's cow for kicking over a lantern.

66
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:04,020
Decades later, in 1893,

67
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:08,120
reporter Michael Ahern admitted he had
invented the cow's story for color.

68
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:09,800
And in 1997,

69
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:13,040
the Chicago City Council
officially exonerated Mrs.

70
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:14,280
O 'Leary and her cow.

71
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:18,560
Yet while telegraphs flashed Chicago's
disaster to every newspaper on Earth,

72
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:23,340
something far more horrifying had already
wiped a Wisconsin town off the map.

73
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:29,500
At the exact same hour, Chicago's
alarm bells began ringing.

74
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:34,660
The thriving lumber town of Peshtigo,
Wisconsin, home to about 1700 residents

75
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:39,040
and the largest woodenwear factory in
the country, faced a true meteorological

76
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:40,080
firestorm.

77
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:44,400
The howling southwest front merged
dozens of woodland slash fires into a

78
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:48,360
towering wall of flame generating
hurricane -force fire tornadoes and

79
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:51,360
temperatures estimated above
2,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

80
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:55,480
French Catholic priest Father Peter
Pernan lived through that night and

81
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:57,560
recorded an unforgettable
eyewitness memoir.

82
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:01,820
He described a deafening roar sweeping
through the treetops before glowing

83
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:05,980
embers rain down like snow, turning
the town into an oven in less than an

84
00:05:06,020 --> 00:05:10,200
hour. Hundreds rushed into the icy
Peshtigo River, ducking their heads

85
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:13,360
underwater every few seconds to keep
their hair and shoulders from catching

86
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:16,020
fire while burning timbers
drifted beside them.

87
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:20,240
Even in the water, many drowned or
succumbed to smoke and freezing shock.

88
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:25,360
By dawn, Peshtigo was gone, not just
damaged, erased down to twisted iron

89
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:26,620
machinery and white ash.

90
00:05:27,420 --> 00:05:31,740
Across both sides of Green Bay, the
firestorm burned roughly 1.2 million

91
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:35,080
acres, an area larger than the
entire state of Rhode Island.

92
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:39,380
Because whole frontier families vanished
along with county records, historians

93
00:05:39,420 --> 00:05:43,240
